ACID is an acronym representing the four fundamental properties—Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability—that a database system
must guarantee to ensure transactions are processed reliably. 
It is the gold standard that ensures your data remains valid and uncorrupted, 
even in the face of power failures, network crashes, or thousands of concurrent users.

Technical Breakdown :-

Atomicity ('All or Nothing'): A transaction is treated as a single, indivisible unit. If a transaction consists of five steps,
and step four fails, the database automatically rolls back the first three steps. Example: In a bank transfer,
if I deduct $100 from Account A, but the system crashes before crediting Account B, Atomicity ensures the $100 is returned to Account A.

Consistency ('Valid State to Valid State'): A transaction can only bring the database from one valid state to another.
It enforces all predefined rules, such as constraints, cascades, and triggers. 
Example: If a database rule states an account balance cannot fall below zero, a transaction attempting to withdraw $200 from an account with $100 will fail
and roll back to maintain consistency.

Isolation ('No Interference'): Concurrent transactions occur entirely independently of one another.
The intermediate state of a transaction is invisible to other transactions.
Example: If two people try to withdraw the last $100 from a joint account at the exact same millisecond, Isolation ensures they are sequenced so only one succeeds,
preventing a 'dirty read'.

Durability ('Permanent'): Once a transaction has been successfully committed, it will survive permanently, even if the database server catches fire a millisecond later.
The system achieves this by writing the transaction to a non-volatile transaction log before acknowledging the commit to the user.

Buisness Context :- 
Choosing a database with strong ACID guarantees is critical for operational (OLTP) systems like financial ledgers, inventory management,
or e-commerce checkouts. If you sacrifice ACID properties for raw speed or availability—which many NoSQL databases do by
defaulting to 'eventual consistency'—you risk overselling inventory or losing financial records during a network partition.

Design Decision :-

Historically, ACID properties were exclusively the domain of relational operational databases like PostgreSQL or MySQL.
Data Lakes were the 'wild west' where partial failures left dirty data behind.
Today, as a data engineer, I leverage open table formats like Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, or Apache Hudi.
These formats bring full ACID transactional guarantees directly to the Data Lake (forming a Lakehouse),
which means I can safely have Spark clusters writing massive batches of data at the exact same time my BI analysts are querying
it without any risk of them seeing corrupted, half-written data.
